.TH DIPISCAN 1 "2026-08-27" "dvbipitools" "dvbipitools Manual"
.SH NAME
dipiscan \- scan and report on DVB-IPI multicast streams
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dipiscan
[\fIoptions\fR] 1>\fIplaylist\fR 2>\fIlog\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dipiscan
sweeps a multicast /24 (or the analogous IPv6 range) for DVB-IPI services
and writes a playlist of what answered. Every address/port combination in
the configured range is probed once; each probe joins the multicast group
(directly via IGMP/MLD, or through an HTTP TS proxy) and waits for a PAT
and SDT service name within the configured time budget.
.PP
This tool exists because deployments do not always use DVBSTP/SD&S
service discovery as implemented by
.BR dipisds (1).
Where SD&S is available, scanning a live multicast range for services is
unnecessary; \fBdipiscan\fR fills the gap when it is not.
.PP
Output can be written as an M3U playlist, XSPF playlist, a local CSV
format, SD&S Broadcast Discovery XML (ETSI TS 102 034), or discarded
entirely (only the stderr log remains). The M3U, XSPF, and CSV formats
are convenience additions and are not part of the DVB-IPI specification.
Every format also carries the DVB triplet (transport_stream_id,
original_network_id, service_id) read from PAT/SDT: CSV as three extra
columns, M3U as \fItsid\fR/\fIonid\fR/\fIsid\fR attributes on the
.B #EXTINF
line, XSPF as an
.B <extension application="urn:dvbipitools:dvb-triplet">
element per track, XML as the
.B DVBTriplet
element the schema already defines for it.
.PP
.B \-f xml
also needs \fB\-P\fR (\fB\-\-provider\fR): the schema's DomainName is
required, and dipiscan has no identity of its own to put there. Feed
the result straight into
.B "dipisds \-a \-i scan.xml \-m ..."
to announce it.
.PP
By default one address is reported as one program: whichever program's
PMT resolves first (an MPTS address still answers, just with only one of
its programs found). With \fB\-M\fR, every program listed in the PAT at
that address gets its own playlist entry, same URI, distinct
\fIsid\fR/name.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mcast " \fIaddr\fR"
Base multicast group, IPv4 or IPv6; the last byte is swept 1..254.
Default: 239.19.75.0.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-port " \fIport\fR[\-\fIport\fR]"
Port or inclusive port range. Default: 8700.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " \fIfmt\fR"
Output format: m3u, csv, xspf, xml, or null. Default: m3u.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-provider " \fIname\fR"
DomainName for the output. Required when \fB\-f xml\fR is used.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-out " \fIpath\fR"
Output file, or "\-" for stdout. Default: stdout. Progress and
\fB\-v\fR diagnostics always go to stderr, regardless of \fB\-o\fR.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout " \fIsecs\fR"
Wall-clock time budget per candidate for a named result (PAT + SDT
service name). 1..3600. Default: 1. If a candidate produces no packets
at all within 300 ms of the join, it is skipped early, without waiting
out the full budget.
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-mpts
Report every PAT-listed program at an address, not just the first.
Changes what \fB\-t\fR waits for: instead of exiting as soon as any one
program is named, the probe waits for every PAT-listed program to be
named, up to the full \fB\-t\fR budget. A program whose PMT resolved but
whose SDT name did not arrive in time is still reported, as "(no SDT)";
one whose PMT never resolved is left out of that pass entirely. Raise
\fB\-t\fR if a scanned mux carries several programs and rows go missing
or come back unnamed.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-http\-proxy " \fIip\fR:\fIport\fR"
Probe through an HTTP TS proxy instead of a direct IGMP/MLD join. Port
defaults to 80 if omitted.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-http\-path " \fItmpl\fR"
Request path template used against the proxy, \fB\-u\fR only.
.B %g
expands to the group address,
.B %p
to the port,
.B %%
to a literal "%"; any other character after "%" is invalid. Default:
.IR /udp/%g:%p/ .
Ignored (with a warning) if given without \fB\-u\fR.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-iface " \fIiface\fR"
Interface to use for the multicast join. Default: kernel default route,
usually wrong when multi-homed.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print per-candidate packet counts on stderr, in addition to the name.
.TP
.B \-\-color \fIwhen\fR
Control colored output: auto, always, or never. Default: auto.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH STOPPING
SIGINT or SIGTERM stops the scan after the current candidate and closes
the playlist properly.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B dipiscan \-m 239.19.75.0 \-p 8700-8705 >hd.m3u
Scan a custom address/port range, write an M3U playlist to a file.
.TP
.B dipiscan \-v \-f csv \-o scan.csv
Scan with per-candidate diagnostics on stderr, write CSV output.
.TP
.B dipiscan \-u 127.0.0.1:8080 \-m 239.19.75.0 \-f xspf >playlist.xspf
Scan through an HTTP TS proxy, write an XSPF playlist.
.TP
.B dipiscan \-f xml \-P example.org \-o scan.xml
Write SD&S Broadcast Discovery XML, ready to feed into \fBdipisds \-a \-i\fR.
.TP
.B dipiscan \-M \-t 3 \-f xml \-P example.org \-o scan.xml
MPTS-aware scan: one row per program per address, with a raised timeout
budget to allow every PAT-listed program to be named.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including no options given: usage hint printed, nothing else
done).
.TP
.B nonzero
Argument error, output file could not be opened, or the scan was
interrupted by a signal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dipisds (1),
.BR dipixy (1)
.PP
